          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Belief centrally means personal trust, reliance, and commitment to God or Christ for salvation, not mere intellectual assent; obedience and works are commonly treated as its expression, evidence, or necessary accompaniment.\",\"book_summary\":\"Belief means receiving God’s testimony as both true and authoritative, allowing it to govern conduct, and therefore acting upon it; persistent disobedience reveals unbelief despite factual assent.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Saving belief is personal trust and committed reliance on God or Christ beyond bare assent.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Genuine belief is obedient reception of God’s authoritative testimony beyond bare assent.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book more sharply excludes purported belief that does not issue in obedience. The consensus also rejects mere assent, but often permits distinctions between faith itself and obedience as its fruit, evidence, or completion; the book places enacted submission closer to the defining boundary of genuine belief.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no replacement of the central referent: both identify genuine belief as a committed response to God rather than mere cognition. The material difference is whether obedience is constitutive of belief or a necessary consequence and evidence of it.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus category of committed, non-merely-intellectual faith but specifies it as receiving divine testimony as governing and acting accordingly. This is already recognizable in the consensus alternatives concerning allegiance, obedience, and faith completed by works, so it is not novel. It materially narrows the extension by reclassifying persistently disobedient assent as unbelief rather than genuine belief, making REFINED more appropriate than MATCH or DIVERGENT.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"John 12:42–43 explicitly calls the non-confessing rulers believers, and James 2 distinguishes faith from the works that complete or demonstrate it. These texts could support distinguishing deficient or dead belief from obedience rather than defining all genuine belief by action, so the book may compress categories that many interpreters keep analytically distinct.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
